    "lawCode": "Amd §7211, rpld §7212, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7986A\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to continuing education\nfor professional engineers, land surveyors and professional geologists;\nand to repeal section 7212 of the education law relating to mandatory\ncontinuing education for land surveyors\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nUpdate continuing education laws for professional engineers and land\nsurveyors and to extend the law to professional geologists.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nRequires geologists, who first became licensed in 2016, to complete 36\nhours of continuing education requirements and consolidates existing\ncontinuing education requirements for geologists, engineers and land\nsurveyors into one section of law.  Modernizes existing continuing\neducation requirements by eliminating the restriction on educational\nactivity format. Defines the courses and activities that are acceptable\nto the department and allows for six hours of continuing education to\ncarry over to a subsequent triennium. Repeals section 7212 of the Educa-\ntion Law.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nThe amended version simply deletes the word \"engineers\" from line 7 of\npage 1 in the original version, because the bill addresses three\nprofessions.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nLegislation is needed in order to modernize outdated laws that regulate\ncontinuing education for professional engineers and land surveyors, and\nrequire continuing education for professional geologists. Section 7211\nof the Education Law, which regulates continuing education for profes-\nsional engineers, was enacted in 2002.  Since that time, such factors as\na burgeoning use of on-line educational opportunities has revolutionized\nthe manner in which continuing education programs are offered. This bill\nseeks to allow the State Board of Engineering, Land Surveying and Geolo-\ngy and the professions that it regulates to take full advantage of these\nopportunities. Combining the three professions in a single section of\nlaw also eliminates redundancy and promotes efficiency in administering\nthe law. The carry-over provision is consistent with the laws in other\nstates and laws that regulate other professions in New York.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nJanuary 1, 2022\n",
